## Interfaces and UX

You can define multiple “faces” for the same engine so different people can engage at their own comfort level.

- Conversation interface  
  - Chat-style interface where users ask questions and see multi-brain responses plus explanations and visual trade‑off maps.  
  - Great for early experimentation and internal usage.

- Decision templates  
  - Predefined decision flows (e.g., “New feature approval”, “Hiring senior engineer”, “Raising capital”) that auto-collect required inputs and run standard multi-brain pipelines.  
  - Helps non-expert users follow best-practice decision processes without knowing the internal architecture.

- Dashboard and notebooks  
  - A dashboard showing current world-model state and recent decisions with outcomes; allows exploration of “what-if” scenarios.  
  - Notebook-style interface where power users can design custom multi-brain workflows, selecting which brains and leader profiles to include.

***

## Governance and guardrails

Agentic architectures emphasize explainability, human oversight, and integration with existing processes.[11] You can bake these in from the start.

- Human-in-the-loop tiers  
  - Define risk tiers: low (auto-approve with logging), medium (human review recommended), high (human review required).  
  - Tag each decision with a tier and route accordingly.

- Ethical and policy boundaries  
  - Codify non-negotiable constraints (e.g., legal compliance, safety, fairness) as “hard rules” that override any leader’s advice.[3][11]
  - Make these constraints part of the meta-brain’s global policy layer.

- Organizational integration  
  - Map where Dev-Brain plugs into your real workflows: product planning, engineering reviews, budgeting, research planning.[11]
  - Each integration gets its own playbook describing inputs, outputs, and audit requirements.

